Dear Gentoo/Alpha geeks
Currently I am willing to bring an API CS20D AlphaServer=2C fitted with two=
 EV68AL=2C back to life. I can access this very compact but very loud machi=
ne through a serial console=2C and ultimately get a remote display with VNC=
 on my iMac hopefuly in a very near future.
One last trick to solve: ABOOT starts the kernel=2C and the initrd goes up =
to the selection of the keymap. Then=2C it says:Code:!! The root block devi=
ce is unspecified or not detected.   Please specify a device to boot=2C or =
"shell" for a shell...boot() ::
Why do I get there?=20
Because of the simplistic hardware configuration=2C there is no possibility=
 to connect a display=2C and it is hard to plug the box over the internet. =
Previously=2C I did start the installation from the usual minimal iso image=
=2C however the process described in the documents=2C using standard emerge=
 commands was too lengthy and leave the machine in an unknown state because=
 the compilation stopps in the middle of nowhere. So=2C I choose to start f=
rom the LiveCD available for download on the official website http://distfi=
les.gentoo.org/experimental/alpha/livecd/.
Obviously=2C the way Gentoo works with packages allows us to do so=2C after=
 all the simple "emerge --update --deep world" can do the trick to bring th=
e live system back to the current stand. Since the current stand is moving =
quite fast these days=2C and because the settings of the compiler suite and=
 compilable source can be a painful process that can leave this noisy and p=
ower hungry server alive for days. I think this can be a good method to cop=
e to underlying problems of the very fluidic and fast changing Gentoo distr=
ibution.
Doing so=2C I could finaly step over the Chapter One of Gentoo Handbook=2C =
and start with real things which are unfortunately only briefly discribed i=
n the handbooks available on internet. But obviously=2C there is something =
not clear in the documentation=2C or at least the information is not well s=
orted=2C how Gentoo is Booting on AlphaServers.
There is no other example of aboot.conf than the one delivered with the dis=
tribution itself=2C so I took it and simply copied into etc/aboot.conf of t=
he target system. This brought the Gentoo box to a semi-finished product: A=
fter a short cp /mnt/livecd sda1 I was pretty much ready with the basic pro=
grams. The following aboot.conf summarize all trials
0:/boot/gentoo initrd=3D/boot/gentoo.igz root=3D/dev/sda1 init=3D/linuxrc  =
dokeymap console=3DttyS01:/boot/gentoo initrd=3D/boot/gentoo.igz root=3D/de=
v/sda2 init=3D/linuxrc  dokeymap console=3DttyS03:/boot/gentoo initrd=3D/bo=
ot/gentoo.igz root=3D/dev/ram0 init=3D/linuxrc  dokeymap looptype=3Dsquashf=
sloop=3D/image.squashfs4:/boot/gentoo initrd=3D/boot/gentoo.igz root=3D/dev=
/ram0 init=3D/linuxrc  dokeymap looptype=3Dsquashfsloop=3D/image.squashfs c=
onsole=3DttyS0(*) of course all files mentionned here were copied into thei=
r proper locations

I had a look in the MAN Pages of aboot.conf=2C abootconf and aboot=2C but t=
he explanation is rather simplistic. I also read the threads regarding prob=
lem configuring the bootloader and Gentoo aboot issues on alpha ev56=2C but=
 I don't think we are in these cases. Thinking I just lack one parameter in=
 my aboot.conf=2C it would be very interesting if someone would extend the =
explanation in the man pages=2C to include an explanation about the last pa=
rameter "cdroot" and also the meaning of "looptype" "zisofs". Perhaps I nee=
d to flash the kernel is the first sectors of the harddrive=2C like it is d=
one with fd0 boot disks=2C using the brute-force DD command! What else will=
 I miss=2C the configuration of EMERGE perhaps ?
